                  "id": "1762443090523",
                  "inputs": [
                    {
                      "id": "1762443090527_1",
                      "name": "prompt",
                      "type": "string",
                      "value": "You are a highly intelligent Expert in analysing the given Oracle Service Cloud analytics XML definition. Your task is to generate a JSON response from the input xml definition for a report.\n\n### Step 1: Extract XML content\n- Extract the **XML definition**  as `{{$context.$nodes.REPORT_LAYOUT_EXTRACTOR_LOOP.$currentItem.fileDetails.fileContent}}`.\n\n### Step 2: Get the Different types of field elements in the XML definition\n- From the XML definition get all `cols_item` under `<cols>`, extract the `val` property and generate a list of string\n\n### Step 3: Set the report type property\nGet the alias property of output as the value of tables->table_item->alias element and set the reportType from below mapping of alias\n- contacts -> Contact\n\n### Step 4: Set the column property\nSet the generated JSON with following structure as columns property of output:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Extracted `Val` of element\",\n  \"order\": \"Extracted `display_order` of element\",\n  \"colId\": \"Extracted`col_id` of element\"\n}\n```\n\n### Step 5: Set the type property\nSet the type property as 'Report'\n\n### Step 6: Set the filter property\nSet the filter property by extracting the `filter` nodes under `filters` element and map the properties as below:\n    - value -> val2\n    - key -> val1\n        -   Do the following step for getting the key from <val1>:\n        -   convert the <val1> tag value by splitting at the period, the first part is entity name and second part is field name.\n        -   `{{$context.$variables.fieldMapping}}` contains a map of entities and each entity has the mapping for field name.\n        -   Identify the most matching property from `{{$context.$variables.fieldMapping}}` and from the value identify the field mapping\n        -   filtering field will be the result from above step.\n    - type -> data_type\n    - operation -> oper\n    - filterId -> fltr_id\n    - revision -> revision\n\n### Step 7: Set the id and Name\nSet the below properties:\n- id as `{{$context.$nodes.REPORT_LAYOUT_EXTRACTOR_LOOP.$currentItem.id}}`\n- name as `{{$context.$nodes.REPORT_LAYOUT_EXTRACTOR_LOOP.$currentItem.name}}`\n\n### Step 8: Set the Field Expression\nConvert the value of <fltr_expr> to a readable format which is a postfix (reverse Polish) logical expression that describes how to combine the filters, using &  |. For example: \n    - `1 2 |` to `1 OR 2`\n    - `1 2 &` to `1 AND 2`\n    - `1 3 2 | 4 | 5 &` to `(((1 OR 3 OR 2) OR 4) AND 5)`\nSet the generated value as a json property `filterExpression`\n\n## Output the json\nOutput the generated json\n\n## Guidelines\n- Use only the XML definition data - never fabricate information\n- Do not display tool or function names in user-facing messages"
                    },
